omplex  important issues involving inequality  race access to healthcare workplace  protections and social justice but a lot  of the time they imply that we can solve  these issues just by changing the  ingredients in your beauty products for  this episode they say consumers can act  by avoiding all talc products which as  we talked about earlier isn't a  recognized risk factor they don't  mention any of the other better  recognized preventative things you could  do that medical organizations actually  recommend they also recommend using apps  like skin deep detox me and cleria which  rate and rank product safety and lists  dangerous ingredients  so it's really pushing clean beauty apps  and clean beauty logic where ingredients  can be neatly divided into good and bad  and you can work out how safe a product  is based on just the ingredients list  i've talked about this before it is a  really clever marketing technique but  it's just not the way the world works  how safe an ingredient is depends on how  you're exposed to it this is a very  basic principle in toxicology the most  important factor is usually how much  there is a concept that's commonly  referred to as the dose makes the poison  the ingredients list usually doesn't  have many numbers clean beauty apps and  websites also often misinterpret studies  they'll take studies on cells and  animals where they might have fed the  ingredient to rats or injected it into  them  and they just assume that they have the  same health effects in humans and the  thing is proper trained toxicologists  have actually analyzed the same studies  before and published their conclusions  and cosmetic chemists use ingredients  and products based on their  recommendations so on one side you have  scientists with years and years of  specialized knowledge saying that these  ingredients are safe when used in  certain ways you also have formulators  who know where they got their  ingredients from they know the  impurities in them they know how much  they put into the product and they know  these safe recommendations and then on  the other side you have these clean  beauty organizations that don't know how  much is in the product or where the  ingredient comes from saying that it is  just blanket unsafe plus these  organizations seem not to know that  humans aren't cells in a petri dish or  rats and really just the basics of  toxicology the two sides are just really  not equal and then to recommend clean  beauty as the solution to these complex  social problems  it makes me really frustrated because  these issues really need to be solved  and the world research solutions are  complex and a lot of people are working  on them but not so pretty are really  emphasizing these oversimplified fake  solutions that aren't supported by the  evidence so they won't solve these  issues they could just act as a  distraction and make things worse they  also make consumers waste a whole bunch  of money by throwing out perfectly good  products and buying even more and a lot  of the ways this episode brings up these  issues in the first place  just don't really make sense for example  one of the lawyers said that jnj's baby  powder marketing targeted  african-american women that were  overweight that lived in the south  which is made up by the documentary to  be kind of sinister and predatory and  that according to lawsuits it has  something to do with the fact that this  demographic would be less likely to get  an early diagnosis and again inequality  of access to healthcare due to race and  other socio-economic factors is a really  big problem but demographic based  marketing is standard people from  different cultural backgrounds use  different products and one of the big  uses of baby powder is for reducing  shaving between the thighs in hot and  sweaty weather  so it seems like there's a much more  straightforward explanation that doesn't  involve a huge evil conspiracy the  documentary also says that j and j they  are still making a product in south  africa  in colombia and in india so they're  really targeting women of color  with  a product that could be carcinogenic but  j j's talc baby powder is also still  sold in a whole bunch of very white  european countries like ireland  switzerland germany and norway which  goes against this whole targeting women  of color theory so the good points i  think this episode made i completely  agree with this bit there is really no  government authority that is checking to  see whether the products are actually  safe i think a lot of us have seen posts  on social media about products with mold  product textures changing in concerning  ways products with dishonest advertising  like filters and illegal drug claims  some of us have even submitted  complaints to regulators and realized  that  very few of these get dealt with in any  official sort of way this isn't just a  problem in the us it's a problem pretty  much everywhere and the fda does  actually do more than a lot of other  countries the eu system tends to get  recommended by a lot of clean beauty  advocates  but on this specific issue their  regulators haven't actually publicly  investigated asbestos or published any  surveillance testing like the fda  studies i think it's a pretty crap  situation that a regular consumer had to  test the claire's makeup it just seems  like the sort of issue that regulators  should be more proactive in tackling i  also agree that the texas two-step  scheme that j and j are using sounds  pretty crappy it's a legal loophole that  allows companies to massively reduce  payouts in lawsuits and it seems  unsurprising that companies are going to  try to reduce their losses as much as  they legally can  hopefully legislation will close this  loophole so my takeaways first off  asbestos doesn't seem to be a common  issue in cosmetics according to better  evidence that the documentary seemed to  purposely ignore if you're still  uncomfortable using talc maybe avoid  using talcum powder this is one of the  bigger sources of talc and cornstarch  based alternatives work pretty well the  amount of talc and makeup that you  breathe in is pretty small to begin with  and based on those fda surveys asbestos  in makeup doesn't seem to be a big issue  more importantly if you're worried about  asbestos be aware of the bigger risks  like your home and any other buildings  you regularly go to i've left some  linked to medical websites in the  description when watching documentaries  be really alert about the ways that they  might be manipulating you some things  are hard to spot unless you already know  the topic but in a lot of documentaries  one obvious red flag is which experts  they use so ask yourself what experts  are they using are they the right  experts for the topic they're discussing  are there obvious categories of experts  missing if so look up what these experts  are saying i'll be talking about the  other episodes soon so subscribe and  click the notification bell for that  also hit the like and leave a comment if  you enjoyed this breakdown the full  convo from our watch party is a podcast  on the eco world there's also a great  book called getting risk right which i  really recommend if you want to get your  head around how to not get on that panic  wagon when these sorts of issues come up
 

